---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { seo } from '../lib/content';
import { defaultSeo } from '../lib/blocks';
import { DOORS } from '../consts';

const prototype = DOORS[2];
const capability = DOORS[3];

// Editable from the joshpenzell.com admin; falls back to the default above.
const meta = await seo('ia.seo.consulting', defaultSeo('ia.seo.consulting'));
---

<Base
  title={meta.title}
  description={meta.description}
  path="/transformation-consulting/"
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Build the proof. Transfer the practice.</p>
    <h1>Prototypes and capability</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Turn direction into something people can try, then make the capability part of how
      your team works.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap page-body">
    <p><strong>Prototype outcome.</strong> {prototype.outcome}</p>
    <p><strong>Capability outcome.</strong> {capability.outcome}</p>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Inspiration has a short half-life</h2>
      <p>
        A keynote can change what a room can see. It cannot change what happens on
        Thursday afternoon in a workflow nobody in the room owns. That gap is where most
        change programs quietly stall—not because people resisted, but because nothing about
        the actual work was ever redesigned.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the engagement for organizations past the inspiration stage and willing to
        change behavior, workflow, learning, or operating practice.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule" id="build">
      <p class="eyebrow">Prototype sprints</p>
      <h2>Build something real enough to disagree with</h2>
      <p>
        We do not open with an eighteen-month roadmap. We open by finding the smallest
        real piece of work where a change would be visible, and running it somewhere the
        cost of being wrong is low.
      </p>
      <p>Each experiment is designed to produce three things:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Evidence</strong> — something observed, not asserted.</li>
        <li><strong>Trust</strong> — a team that has now done the thing once.</li>
        <li><strong>Direction</strong> — a clearer read on what should scale and what should not.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Scale follows evidence. That order is the whole method, and it is the part that is
        genuinely uncomfortable, because it means committing to fewer things earlier and
        finding out sooner when one of them is wrong.
      </p>
      <h3>What a sprint can produce</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A working learning experience or interactive program</li>
        <li>A decision tool, dashboard, or evidence model</li>
        <li>An AI-enabled workflow or automation with human judgment designed in</li>
        <li>A functional prototype that makes the larger idea testable</li>
        <li>A facilitation or operating playbook the internal team can run</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule" id="own">
      <p class="eyebrow">Capability studios</p>
      <h2>The goal is not to need us forever</h2>
      <p>
        Some work needs more than a sprint. A Capability Studio is an ongoing cadence of
        working jams, coaching, guided builds, and interpretation sessions. We stay close
        enough to help the practice take root and step back as your team becomes able to
        direct the work itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        The handoff is not a final deck. It is people who have rehearsed the moves on their
        own material, artifacts they helped build, and a repeatable way to decide what to do next.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section-rule">
      <h2>Where the work often lands</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Adoption that has plateaued after a tool rollout</li>
        <li>Learning and enablement programs measured by completion rather than by change</li>
        <li>Workflows where AI has been added beside the work instead of into it</li>
        <li>Teams competent in isolation and misaligned together</li>
        <li>Important ideas stuck in slides because nobody has built the first believable version</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="pull">Stop explaining the future long enough to rehearse a piece of it.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start a conversation</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-we-work/">See the method</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
